the Department of Homeland Security is  demanding airlines and airports  worldwide tighten their security so  terrorists cannot smuggle bombs onto  planes now those changes will force more  than 300,000 passengers a day to deal  with new security measures and screening  technology officials say the alternative  would be to widen a ban on carry-on  electronics that now affects eight  countries in Africa and the Middle East  Chris Van Cleve is at Reagan National  Airport outside Washington Chris good  morning  well good morning these heightened  security measures will affect 180  airlines flying about 2100 flights a day  to the US and comes amid growing concern  about terrorists targeting aviation  especially with laptop based bombs that  may be able to get past some airport  screening we cannot play in a national  whack-a-mole with each new threat the  Department of Homeland Security told  airlines serving about 280 airports in  105 countries basically everywhere US  airliners fly from to ratchet up  security or face a potential electronics  band that means flyers coming to the  United States should expect to see  increased scrutiny including canines and  potential secondary screening especially  of laptops let me be clear security is  my number-one concern  our enemies are adaptive and we have to  be adaptive as well this new security  burden will largely fall on Airlines the  group representing US carriers supports  increased security but complained this  decision should have been subject to a  greater degree of collaboration and  coordination as to not inconvenience the  traveling public you can make a really  secure aviation system but nobody's  flying if you do that peter neffenger is  the former TSA administrator it doesn't  surprise me that that we've gotten to a  point where we say you know we think  we've got to do something a little bit  different particularly given that  certain groups have been working on  developing devices to put into laptops  DHS has called for improving detection  equipment to combat the threat of bombs  hidden inside laptops this month the  agency started testing high-definition  three  the CT scanners for carry-on bags at  Phoenix Sky Harbor Airport the bad guy  is very agile and always changing where  we need to be that agile that test  program is set to expand to Boston by  the end of the month and likely other  cities in the coming weeks now  DHS did not disclose what these new  enhanced security measures are but the  airline's say they'll begin implementing  at least some of them within the next  three weeks  they are expecting some disruptions and  delays for fliers Gail all right that's  not good news thank you very much Chris
 

